INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - KITCHEN - MORNING

There are flat-pack boxes on the floor. Through the window we can see the estuary. There is a tide table pinned to the wall.

JUNE, fifties, stands in the middle of the room in her coat.

PAUL, forties, comes in with a roll of tape.

PAUL
Hello, June. It has been two years since we last spoke, because we are estranged.

JUNE
Hello, Paul. Yes, we are estranged. I have always felt that you were Mum's favourite and that has caused a lot of resentment in me over the years.

PAUL
I know. I have always known that. But you were the one who moved away, so it is partly your own fault.

JUNE
Maybe. Anyway, the surveyor is coming at four o'clock, so we have to clear the whole house today. If we do not clear the house today, the sale will fall through, and that would be very bad.

PAUL
Yes, that would be very bad. We should get started.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - KITCHEN - LATER

June and Paul sort things into piles.

PAUL
Keep, tip, or give to Elspeth next door.

JUNE
I do not know. You decide. You always decided everything.

PAUL
Okay. I will decide.

He puts things in piles. June watches him do it.

JUNE
It is very sad being in this house. It reminds me of my childhood, and of Mum, and how much I miss her.

PAUL
Yes, it is very sad. I miss her too.

They keep sorting for a while.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - DAY

There is a bureau against the wall. It is locked.

JUNE
This bureau is locked. Where is the key?

PAUL
I do not know. Maybe it is lost.

JUNE
That is a shame. I wonder what is inside it.

PAUL
Probably just old papers.

JUNE
You are probably right.

She stops trying to open the bureau and sits down.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY

June makes a cup of tea. Paul comes in.

PAUL
I found the key to the bureau. It was in a tea tin on the shelf.

JUNE
Oh, well done. You always were good at finding things.

PAUL
Shall I open it?

JUNE
Yes, you open it.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - DAY

Paul opens the bureau. Inside there is a cardboard box with a crematorium label on it, and some paperwork.

PAUL
These are Mum's ashes. And this is the contract for the sale of the house. I signed it three weeks before she died, because she asked me to.

JUNE
I see. That is quite a big thing to find out.

PAUL
I should have told you. I am sorry.

JUNE
It is okay. I forgive you.

PAUL
Thank you.

JUNE
We should probably do something with the ashes at some point.

PAUL
Yes, at some point.

They leave the box on the bureau.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - CONTINUOUS

June and Paul keep packing boxes.

JUNE
Do you remember how Mum always said you must never go out on a falling tide?

PAUL
Yes, I remember. She said it every summer.

JUNE
She said a lot of things.

PAUL
She did.

They tape up another box.

EXT. ESTUARY HOUSE - STEPS - DAY

June goes outside and sits on the stone steps. The tide is going out. The old rowing boat is lying on its side in the mud.

JUNE
That was our boat when we were children. It has not been used in years.

She looks at it for a while and then goes back inside.

EXT. MUD FLAT - DAY

Paul comes out and stands next to the boat.

PAUL
This boat has a hole in it now. We could never take it out anyway.

JUNE
That is a shame. I would have liked to go out on the water one last time before we sell the house.

PAUL
So would I. But it is not possible, unfortunately.

JUNE
No, I suppose not.

PAUL
It is quite sad, really, when you think about it. All the things you mean to do and then never do.

JUNE
That is very true. I feel the same way about Mum. There were a lot of things I meant to say to her.

PAUL
I am sure she knew.

JUNE
I hope so.

They go back inside together.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - KITCHEN - LATE AFTERNOON

June and Paul stand among the packed boxes. Everything is cleared.

PAUL
Well, we did it. The house is empty.

JUNE
Yes. It feels strange.

PAUL
It does feel strange.

JUNE
I think this has brought us closer together.

PAUL
I think so too. Family is very important.

JUNE
It really is.

EXT. ESTUARY HOUSE - STEPS - LATE AFTERNOON

June and Paul sit on the steps. A car pulls up on the road above.

PAUL
That will be the surveyor.

JUNE
Yes.

PAUL
Well. Goodbye, June. Let us not leave it two years next time.

JUNE
Let us not.

He goes up to the road. June sits on the step by herself and looks out at the water. The cardboard box is still on the bureau inside the empty house.
